; apply and call-with-current-continuation are FunctionObj subclasses with
; custom call/tailCall (not PrimitiveObj); dataless arms here.
define ApplyFnObj ()
define CallCCFnObj ()

; A DSSSL node-list (Grove Inc 4): an ordered view over a node buffer from
; `start`. A single node is a length-1 node-list; the empty node-list has
; start == the buffer length. node-list-rest bumps `start` (cheap views).
;
; LAZY CHARACTER EXPANSION. The child/descendant/sibling axes hand out one node
; PER CHARACTER of a data run (Grove's chunk/view split), which as a flat array
; costs a pointer per character — the reference pays nothing because
; NodeListObj::nodeListChunkRest iterates LAZILY and skips a whole chunk in one
; step. `prefix` is that protocol in random-access form: when it is non-null the
; buffer holds CHUNK-LEVEL members and prefix[k] is the flat position of member
; k's first character (prefix has one extra sentinel entry, the total). Then
; `start` and the index of node_list_node are flat CHARACTER positions, resolved
; by binary search, and a char view is materialized only when a caller actually
; asks for that position. prefix == null keeps the plain one-node-per-slot
; representation every other producer builds.
;
; ★★★INLINE SINGLETON. `single` holds the node of a ONE-node list directly,
; with `nodes` null — the reference's NodePtrNodeListObj (style/ELObj.h:441),
; which keeps its `NodePtr node_` inline and allocates exactly one collector
; object. Ours used to build an `Array` plus its buffer plus the ELObj: THREE
; region allocations for one node, on the hottest path the engine has.
; Measured 2026-08-06 with tests/dazzle/primprof, styling run: `current-node`
; (708 313 calls) is nothing but a getter plus that construction and cost
; 88.6 ns against the reference's 4.8 ns — 18x, where `+` (one allocation) is
; only 2.5x. The whole node-list family carried the same factor
; (`node-list-first` 26x, `gi` 15x).
;
; The invariant is narrow on purpose: `single <> null` implies `nodes`,
; `prefix` and `gen` are all null, so every accessor answers it with one
; branch and node-list-rest just carries the field along (`start` past 0 makes
; it empty, exactly as `start` past the buffer length does). A producer that
; needs any of the other three keeps building a buffer.
;
; NAMED node lists (NamedNodeList / BaseNamedNodeList) are the same buffer with
; a name-lookup protocol on top, so they ride in this record as `named` rather
; than in an arm of their own — every one of the ten node-list `choose` sites
; would otherwise need a second arm, and a MISSED arm returns 0 in silence.
; The value is NamedNodeList::Type (grove/Node.h:421), which decides BOTH things
; the reference's subclasses vary: the SUBSTITUTION TABLE the lookup normalizes
; through (entitySubstTable for the entity lists, generalSubstTable for every
; other) and WHICH PROPERTY of a member serves as its name — NamedNodeList::
; nodeName dispatches on (list type, node class): an element in an `elements`
; list answers with its ID, an element-type with its GI, everything else with
; its `name` (grove/Node.cxx:691 NodeNameNodeVisitor).
; A DERIVED list (node-list-rest and friends) is a plain node-list in the
; reference too — NamedNodeList is not a NodeList subclass, its `nodeList()`
; hands out an ordinary one — so every deriving constructor leaves this 0.
define NNL_NONE: int 0
define NNL_ELEMENTS: int 1
define NNL_ATTRIBUTES: int 2
define NNL_ENTITIES: int 3
define NNL_NOTATIONS: int 4
define NNL_DOCTYPES_AND_LINKTYPES: int 5
define NNL_ELEMENT_TYPES: int 6
define NNL_ATTRIBUTE_DEFS: int 7
; ★one value BEYOND the reference's enum: the sgml-document class's `entities`
; list is a DocEntitiesNamedNodeList, whose namedNode falls back to the grove's
; defaulted-entity table for a name its own members do not hold. The reference
; distinguishes that by SUBCLASS while carrying type `entities`; here the extra
; value carries it (it behaves as NNL_ENTITIES everywhere else).
define NNL_DOC_ENTITIES: int 8

; LAZY FILTERING (SelectElementsNodeListObj, style/primitive.cxx:5602). The
; reference does not materialize a filtered node-list at all: select-elements
; returns a view that runs the pattern in nodeListFirst / nodeListRest, so a
; caller asking only whether anything matches walks to the FIRST match and
; stops. Ported here as an incremental generator behind the same buffer: `out`
; IS the node-list's `nodes` array, `cursor` the next member of `src` still to
; test, and every accessor extends the buffer just far enough for what it was
; asked (ELObj.force_select). Measured on the DocBook print stylesheets: the
; same 3732 calls step over 0.56 M source members instead of 1.70 M and run
; 1952 pattern matches instead of 3037 — 2.0 % of the styling run's
; instructions, and O(first match) instead of O(n) for the callers that only
; ask whether anything matched.
; `host` is the page the node-list itself lives on, so the deferred matches
; land in exactly the region the eager version used; `src` is a value the
; primitive received, hence never in a region younger than that page.
; The lazy node-list views, all driven through ELObj.force_gen.
; GEN_SELECT: `select-elements` — SelectElementsNodeListObj, `pat` filters `src`.
; GEN_CONCAT: `(node-list a b …)` — the reference's PairNodeListObj (ELObj.cxx
;   :1201), which is a two-pointer cell and copies NOTHING. `src`/`src2` are the
;   two operands; `pat` is unused.
;
; The four AXIS views below are the same mechanism over the four axes that used
; to materialize a full array at construction time. The reference has a lazy
; object for each (style/primitive.cxx): DescendantsNodeListObj for
; `descendants`, SiblingNodeListObj for `preced`/`follow`, and the child axis
; comes straight off the node. `node-list-first` or `node-list-empty?` on one of
; them cost O(1) here instead of O(width of the sibling group), which is what
; the nsweep complexity screen measured as a port defect
; (tests/dazzle/nsweep/README.md: four rows at x3.9 per doubling against the
; reference's x2).
;
; ★All four map over the MEMBERS of their source node-list exactly as the eager
; versions did (the reference wraps itself in a MapNodeListObj for a
; non-singleton argument), so `cursor` is the position in `src` and the
; per-source-node walk state sits in `cur`/`walk`/`sub`/`sub2`/`stop`/`path`.
; ★★A child axis this narrow is cheaper MATERIALIZED than viewed, and the
; number is measured rather than guessed: on the heaviest real-world codegen
; the four axes are asked 11 525 091 times and answer 21 797 328 members —
; 1.9 per call. At that size the generator record and the memo-hosted buffer
; cost more than the members, and the buffer is worse than that: the memo seam
; puts it on the RUN arena, so a view that dies immediately is never given
; back (measured with the frame mark on: +28 % peak). The bound is what keeps
; the asymptotics — anything wider stays lazy, so the complexity screen's rows
; are unaffected (they run 500 to 4000 siblings).
define AXIS_EAGER_MAX: int 8

define GEN_SELECT: int 0
define GEN_CONCAT: int 1
define GEN_CHILDREN: int 2
define GEN_DESCENDANTS: int 3
define GEN_PRECED: int 4
define GEN_FOLLOW: int 5

; A lazy node-list generator. Both kinds live in ONE record rather than in two
; ELObj union arms on purpose: the union's arm size is paid by EVERY ELObj
; allocation in the engine (see the note on SosofoObj), while this record is
; only allocated for a list that actually is a view.
define SelectGen
(
    kind: int
    src: ref[ELObj]
    src2: ref[ELObj]?
    pat: ref[Pattern]?
    interp: ref[Interpreter]
    host: ref[Page]
    out: ref[Array[ref[GroveNode]?]]
    var cursor: int
    var prefix: ref[Array[int]]?
    var total: int
    var cur: ref[GroveNode]?
    var walk: ref[GroveNode]?
    var sub: int
    var sub2: int
    var stop: int
    path: ref[Array[int]]?
    var depth: int
)
{
    ; The record has NO `init` and every construction goes through `make`,
    ; which writes all eighteen fields by name — the shape MacroDef and
    ; GroveNode use. Why not an init: an init is selected by argument COUNT,
    ; and a construction that misses one compiles in silence with garbage in
    ; the field. The 8-field version of this record already paid that once —
    ; because `out` is a local bound to `Array[…]^host()`, the construction did
    ; not resolve to an init at all (the local carries the VALUE type, the
    ; parameter is a pointer), the planner emitted a positional TUPLE over the
    ; fields, and three of four sites got a random cursor and answered "no
    ; matches" (tests/dazzle/grove axis1; fixed 2026-08-02 in
    ; Planner.region_value_matches_pointer, and diagnosed loudly since).
    ;
    ; The walk state, per generator kind:
    ;   SELECT/CONCAT  cursor = the next member of `src` still to test
    ;   every AXIS     cursor = the next flat position of `src` to expand,
    ;                  `cur`  = the source node being expanded (null: fetch the
    ;                           next one), `prefix`/`total` the character-
    ;                           expansion index grown in lockstep with `out`
    ;   CHILDREN       sub    = the next child index of `cur`
    ;   DESCENDANTS    walk   = the last node emitted (null: not started),
    ;                  path/depth = the child index at each level of the walk
    ;   PRECED/FOLLOW  sub    = the next index in the sibling group,
    ;                  stop   = the group's end, walk = the member HOLDING the
    ;                  node, sub2 = the character cursor inside it
    function make(host: ref[Page], kind: int, src: ref[ELObj], src2: ref[ELObj]?, pat: ref[Pattern]?, interp: ref[Interpreter], out: ref[Array[ref[GroveNode]?]], prefix: ref[Array[int]]?, path: ref[Array[int]]?) @ret_explicit_0 true @deep true returns ref[SelectGen] linked




}

; ★FIELD ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING: `start` and `named` are adjacent i32s so that
; LLVM packs them into ONE word. This arm is the largest in the ELObj union
; since the signatures moved behind a pointer, so its width is the width of
; every value in the engine — 4.69 M ELObj allocations per styling run
; (measured, tests/dazzle/primprof), each paying the maximum arm.
define NodeListObj (nodes: ref[Array[ref[GroveNode]?]]?, start: i32, named: i32, prefix: ref[Array[int]]?, gen: ref[SelectGen]?, single: ref[GroveNode]?)
{
    ; ONE init, `gen` and `single` always explicit: a second, shorter overload
    ; would be picked apart by argument COUNT, and a construction that misses
    ; the last argument compiles silently with garbage in the field (CLAUDE.md,
    ; "a call with too many/too few arguments compiles silently"). The IR arity
    ; audit in tests/dazzle/build-cli.sh is the net under that.
    init(nodes: ref[Array[ref[GroveNode]?]]?, start: i32, named: i32, prefix: ref[Array[int]]?, gen: ref[SelectGen]?, single: ref[GroveNode]?) linked








}

; A sosofo (Specification Of a Sequence Of Flow Objects). SosofoObj.cxx is a
; class hierarchy (Append/Literal/ProcessChildren/ProcessNodeList/…); Scaly
; union arms don't subclass, so the arm carries a `kind` discriminator plus two
; slots: `parts` (APPEND's child list) and `aux`, a single ELObj slot reused per
; kind — LITERAL→the string, PROCESS_NODE_LIST→the node-list, PROCESS_NODE→a
; node-singleton node-list. children/trim/next-match carry no data (they use the
; process-time current node + mode). Kept to two pointers so the ELObj union
; does not grow past its other arms (a larger union multiplies every ELObj
; allocation's footprint — it tipped the test region over a page boundary).
; The Style engine (Inc 5) builds these; ProcessContext.process_sosofo walks them.
define SOSO_APPEND: int 0
define SOSO_LITERAL: int 1
define SOSO_PROCESS_CHILDREN: int 2
define SOSO_PROCESS_CHILDREN_TRIM: int 3
define SOSO_PROCESS_NODE_LIST: int 4
define SOSO_PROCESS_NODE: int 5
define SOSO_NEXT_MATCH: int 6
; SOSO_FLOWOBJ — a transform flow object built by `make`. To keep SosofoObj at
; two pointers (below), the characteristics ride in `aux` (a FlowObjBox) and the
; content sosofos in `parts` (like APPEND; empty for atomic flow objects).
define SOSO_FLOWOBJ: int 7
; PageNumberSosofoObj / CurrentNodePageNumberSosofoObj (SosofoObj.h): emit a
; page-number reference through the FOTBuilder (fot: <page-number/>). The
; current-node variant carries its node as a singleton node-list in aux.
define SOSO_PAGE_NUMBER: int 8
define SOSO_CURRENT_NODE_PAGE_NUMBER: int 9
; PageTypeSosofoObj (if-first-page / if-front-page): parts = [match, noMatch],
; aux = an IntegerObj carrying the HF page-type flag (firstHF=1 / frontHF=2).
; Processes nothing when no page type is in effect (outside header/footer).
define SOSO_PAGE_TYPE: int 10
; LabelSosofoObj — wraps a flow object that has a label: (the ctl record with
; the symbol + location rides in the parts slot, see SosofoCtlRec).
define SOSO_LABEL: int 11
; ContentMapSosofoObj — wraps a flow object's content with a content-map:.
define SOSO_CONTENT_MAP: int 12
; DiscardLabeledSosofoObj — the sosofo-discard-labeled primitive: a
; connectable with one port carrying the label and a sink that keeps nothing,
; so whatever inside connects to that label is dropped. The symbol rides in
; the parts slot (a ctl record, like SOSO_LABEL), the content in aux.
define SOSO_DISCARD_LABELED: int 13

; MacroDef — MacroFlowObj::Definition (style/MacroFlowObj.h): the SHARED half
; of a flow-object macro, built once by declare-flow-object-macro and pointed
; at by every FlowObj the macro's `make`s produce. `nics` are the declared
; characteristic names in declaration order, `inits` their default-value
; expressions (null = no default, which evaluates to #f), `contents_id` the
; `#!contents` variable (null = an ATOMIC macro: content is rejected), `body`
; the sosofo expression. `code` is compiled LAZILY on the first process (the
; reference compiles in Definition::process when code_ is null) — the whole
; part must be read before the body's identifiers resolve.
;
; It lives here, next to FlowObj, rather than in Expression: Identifier
; already forward-references Expression/Insn from this module, and FlowObj
; must be able to name the record by pointer.
define MacroDef
(
    nics: ref[Array[ref[Identifier]?]]?
    inits: ref[Array[ref[Expression]?]]
    contents_id: ref[Identifier]?
    body: ref[Expression]?
    var code: ref[Insn]?
)
{
    function make(host: ref[Page], nics: ref[Array[ref[Identifier]?]], inits: ref[Array[ref[Expression]?]], contents_id: ref[Identifier]?, body: ref[Expression]?) @ret_explicit_0 true @deep true returns ref[MacroDef] linked





    ; Definition::isCompound — contentsId_ != 0.
    function is_compound(this: MacroDef) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns bool linked


    function n_nics(this: MacroDef) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns int linked






    ; MacroFlowObj::hasNonInheritedC — a declared characteristic name, by
    ; Identifier POINTER identity (the interning table gives one cell per
    ; name); -1 = not one of ours.
    function nic_index(this: MacroDef, ident: ref[Identifier]?) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns int linked














    function get_code(this: MacroDef) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns ref[Insn]? linked


    procedure set_code(mutable this: ref[MacroDef], c: ref[Insn]) @deep true @deep_this_2 true @deep_whole_2 true linked


    function get_body(this: MacroDef) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns ref[Expression]? linked


    function get_inits(this: MacroDef) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns ref[Array[ref[Expression]?]] linked


    function get_nics(this: MacroDef) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns ref[Array[ref[Identifier]?]] linked


    function get_contents_id(this: MacroDef) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns ref[Identifier]? linked

}

; FlowObj — a transform flow object's evaluated characteristics (the NIC set of
